# EXAMPLE crew script: two GPCs to OPS 0, SPEC 6 up on CRT1, then CRT2
# assigned to GPC2.  Reads the flight software's OWN view of which GPC
# commands each bus (the '*' columns), rather than inferring it from the
# emulator's registers.  Run with NSTS_ANNOUNCE_ROWS=all and screenwatch.py.
# EXAMPLE crew script: two GPCs IPL'd to OPS 0, then CRT2 handed to GPC2 by
# hand.  THE TEST BEHIND IT: with no NBAT entry for an IDP, the lowest
# numbered GPC in that major function commands it (DPS Workbook USA005350
# Rev B, 3.3 DK Assignment), which is why CRT2 reads 'GPC MEMORY 1' after an
# IPL -- GPC1 really is driving it.  A GPC/CRT entry is what moves it.
# Run from discretePanel/ with --gpcs 1,2 --crts 2.
# EXAMPLE crew script: TWO GPCs and two CRTs to OPS 201 -- the configuration
# the vehicle actually flies on orbit.  Run from discretePanel/:
#
#   python3 simulatePASS.py --gpcs 1,2 --crts 2 \
#       --tape ~/workspace/pass-run/OI340700-v44boot.mmv \
#       --script examples/2gpc-ops201.script --duration 900
#
# WHY TWO AND NOT FOUR.  OPS 2 ON ORBIT IS A TWO-GPC SET: the flight MEMORY
# CONFIGURATION TABLE gives CONFIG 2 a GPC SEL of 1,2, with FC strings 1 and 3
# to one computer and 2 and 4 to the other, and the post-insertion procedure
# HALTs the rest.  4gpc-startup.script asks for OPS 201 with four computers,
# which is a mixture that never flies; 4gpc-g3-startup.script is the honest
# four-computer configuration and uses CONFIG 3 / OPS 301.  This file is the
# honest TWO-computer one.
#
# The NBAT item numbering is documented -- CD0001.dfg declares ITEM 7-19 as I1
# range 0-5, and ARFDPSCO.hal:011000BA stores them as CZ2V_STRNG_MC$(ARF_NO-6)
# with a display grouping of 5 + 4 + 4: strings 1-4 plus payload, then CRT 1-4,
# then the launch data buses and MM1/MM2.  See ledger #156, which also records
# that the VALUES in the four-GPC script were composed by us rather than read
# from a procedure; these follow the CONFIG 2 column instead.
#
#     ITEM 1        memory configuration        2
#     ITEM 2,3      GPC SEL                     1, 2   (4 and 5 left unset)
#     ITEM 7-10     FC strings 1-4              1, 2, 1, 2
#     ITEM 11       payload string              1
#     ITEM 12,13    CRT 1, CRT 2                1, 2
#     ITEM 18,19    MM1, MM2                    1, 2
#
# NO SHARED CRT HERE, so the stale-display trap that cost the four-GPC scripts
# three computers does not arise: GPC1 IPLs on CRT1 and GPC2 on CRT2, each on
# its own display.  ipl-one-gpc.script still waits for a NEW screen before
# reading its title, which is what makes that safe in general.

# --- both GPCs to HALT, both IDPs on -----------------------------------------
+0     gpc 1
+0     mode HALT
+0.01  gpc 2
+0.01  mode HALT
+0.02  idppower 1 on
+0.01  idppower 2 on

# --- IPL each computer, on its own CRT and keyboard --------------------------
+0.01  script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=1 crt=1 idp=1 kb=KB1
+5     script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=2 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2

# --- the NBAT for memory configuration 2, then OPS 201 -----------------------


wait crt 2 title "GPC MEMORY" timeout 300
# A wait that cannot be met, so the screen text is logged when it expires.


# SPEC 6 on CRT1: the flight software's own view of who commands each bus.
wait crt 1 title "GPC MEMORY" timeout 300
+5     keys SPEC 6 PRO
wait crt 1 title "GPC/BUS STATUS" timeout 60
+30    subtitle
# then the same after an explicit assignment of CRT2 to GPC2
+5     keys KB2 GPC/CRT 2 2 EXEC
+30    subtitle
wait crt 1 title NEVERMATCHES timeout 60
